diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 56a1a17f..1be0fcbb 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -3,6 +3,29 @@
 ## Unreleased
 
 ### Upgrade steps
+- **Path checks: `hadavin_sdk/pathguard.py` holds the rules for the file operations that the package tripwire test lists.** Before, three separate checks each answered a slightly different question (`config.confined`, the cause-list check, `audit.signer.key_path`) and many file operations that take a path from a command line, a config file or a data file had no check. Now the places named below call `pathguard`. The tripwire test is `tests/test_pathguard_tripwire.py`; the file operations it deliberately leaves out are listed, each with an origin word, the function that checks the path, an exact count and a reason, in `tests/fixtures/pathguard_exceptions.txt`. Each bullet below is one behaviour you may notice.
+  - **The rules (R1 to R13).** A name: not empty and not `.` or `..` (R1); no `/` or `\` (R2); no `:` (R3); no character from the 20 ranges in `pathguard.CONTROL_RANGES`, which include NUL, the C1 controls, zero-width and bidirectional marks, the byte-order mark, the Hangul and Khmer fillers, the braille blank and the musical-symbol format characters (R4); at most 255 bytes (R5); no trailing dot or space (R6); no Windows device name such as `con`, `aux` or `com1` on any operating system (R7); not one of `< > " | ? *` (R12). A path: it must stay inside its folder after links are followed (R8); it must resolve (R10); it must be the right type, file or folder (R11); at most 4096 bytes in all (R5, counted in UTF-8 bytes, not characters); an absolute name is refused as a name (R9, through R2 and R3). A write target that is a folder, or a temp name that is already in use, is refused (R13).
+  - **R3, R6, R7 and R12 apply to every name that goes through `join`, and every read that follows a checked path now uses `join`.** This includes a name an operator typed: the answers file given to `score`, a secret name in a mounted secrets folder, a file name given to `hash_files`, the key files read by `signer`, the golden file of `guard.judge.qualify` and the sign-in token file of the Vault Kubernetes login. On origin/main these were readable whatever their name; now `vault:token`, `con`, `x.` and `a?b` are refused (`PathRefused`, the rule in `PathRefused.rule`, no name in the text; `score` keeps its own wording `answers file is missing or not valid JSON`). A `..` or `.` inside a name is folded first, as before (`hash_files` and the secrets reader read `a/../b` as `b`; a `..` that leaves the folder keeps the wording `path escapes its folder: <name>`). Also now refused at those reads: a file that is a link leaving its own folder (R8; a link that stays inside is read). `pathguard.inside`, `operator_dir`, `operator_file` and `config.confined` still accept those names because no read follows them (the folder-shape checks).
+  - **A refusal from `pathguard` names the argument and the problem, never the value.** The places that still print text the caller gave, exactly as before this change: `config.confined` says `path escapes its folder: <name as written>`, `hash_files` says `not a file: <name>` and `path escapes its folder: <name>`, the mounted secrets reader says `secret '<name>' not found in the mounted folder`, and `signer.build_local` says `no key file at <the key path as typed in the config>`. Two places print a name that came from data and are not changed here: `LocalDirSink` prints the event id when the operating system fails (`intent <id>: <error name>`), and `k1b.bundle.load_bundle` prints the folder name when it does not look like `case-NNN`. The plain message `... does not exist` is exact for the outputs folder, the bundles folder and the hash folder, and the secrets folder says `secrets mounted_file: <folder as written in the config> is not a folder`; the other refusals read `<argument>: <problem>`, for example `outputs folder: contains a control character`.
+  - **An error from the operating system while a typed path is checked is a refusal with a fixed text (R10).** Any error number (for example "File name too long" for a 256-byte name that need not exist), a symbolic-link loop and a NUL are all `PathRefused: <argument>: cannot be resolved`, with no path and no error text. On origin/main and in the earlier draft of this change a raw `OSError` carrying the typed path escaped from `operator_dir`, `operator_file` and `LocalDirSink(root)` for such a name. A sweep of the package found the same class in three more places, now fixed: `signer.key_path` (a key file name too long for the system, or `~name` for a user that does not exist, is a `ValueError` with no path in the text), the tools file name built from an agent id in `registry` (a refusal in words, `PathRefused`), and a tool module name with a part longer than 255 bytes (`ConfigError`).
+  - **What is accepted (so the entry does not claim more).** A name of 255 bytes is accepted by `component`, `join`, `inside` and `operator_file` but refused by `atomic_write`, whose temp name needs 22 more bytes. `inside` accepts `sub/../real.txt` because it resolves inside the folder (`join` refuses a `..` in a name). A link that points inside the folder is accepted by `join`, `inside` and `hash_files`. `component` checks text only, so the name of a link is accepted. `LocalDirSink._object` returns the path for a key that is a link pointing outside without touching it; the read or write that follows refuses it. An audit folder or objects folder that is itself a link is followed (the operator owns it). The 20 ranges of R4 also refuse the joiner and variation characters (U+200D, U+FE0F) and the soft hyphen, so an operator folder named with an emoji sequence is refused.
+  - **`k1b.atomic.atomic_write(path, text)` is now `atomic_write(base, name, text)`.** Pass the folder and the file name separately; there is no old form. The temp file is named `.<name>.<random>.tmp`, is created so that an existing file is never reused (on origin/main the temp name was `<name>.tmp`, and a file already called that was overwritten without a word), is created with mode 0o600 so the finished file is readable by its owner only (on origin/main it was 0o644 with the usual umask), and is removed if the write or the rename fails. A crash that stops the process between the write and the rename still leaves the temp file behind.
+  - **`atomic_write` refuses what it cannot replace, with no path in the message.** A name that is already a link is refused (R8). A name that is a folder is refused (R13); on origin/main it raised a bare `IsADirectoryError` that held the path. A temp name that is already taken is refused (R13) and the existing file is left untouched. A name that is a hard link is replaced by a new file and the other name keeps the old content. A name longer than `MAX_WRITE_NAME_BYTES` (233 bytes) is refused. An operating-system error at the open, the write, the flush or the rename is raised without the temp path and without a chained earlier error, and the temp file is removed.
+  - **`score` reads its `outputs.sha256` digest file only through a checked name.** On origin/main a digest file that was a link leaving the run folder was followed and its content trusted; now it counts as unreadable, not as missing. A run folder or a case file that is a link leaving the outputs folder was already refused on origin/main and still is. A NUL in the outputs folder typed on the command line was a bare `ValueError` (`embedded null byte`) on origin/main; now `score` and `replay` refuse a control character in a folder or file typed on the command line with a plain message and exit code 2 (`--config` with a NUL exited with 3 on origin/main). `replay` checks the run folder again before each file it writes, so a run folder swapped for a link in the middle of a run stops the next write. A swap in the instant between that check and the write itself is not covered.
+  - **`replay.outputs_files` raises a `ConfigError` for a missing outputs folder** (on origin/main it returned an empty list).
+  - **Paths are now the resolved, absolute path.** `ReplayResult.out`, `cfg.root`, `golden_dir`, `inputs_dir` and the paths printed by the commands follow links and no longer depend on the current folder. A relative path given in a config or on the command line is made absolute against the folder the command runs in, once, when it is read.
+  - **`config.confined` refuses a path of more than 4096 bytes and every character of the wider R4 set**, for all of its callers; on origin/main it returned both (a 5000-character name and a name with U+202E). The escape message stays `path escapes its folder: <name>`; the new refusals read `path: too long` and `path: contains a control character`. It does not gain the R3, R6, R7 and R12 rules, because other callers read names from your config. A NUL in the name is now a `ConfigError` (a `PathRefused`); on origin/main it raised a plain `ValueError`, and `ConfigError` is itself a `ValueError`, so an `except ValueError` still catches it.
+  - **`golden_dir` and `inputs_dir` in a config may still point outside the config folder** (the example config uses `../golden`); only their shape is checked, and a value that is not text is a `ConfigError`.
+  - **A bundles folder that holds an entry with a reserved or Windows-invalid name raises `PathRefused` for that entry before the case-id check.** On origin/main the same entry (for example `con`) raised a `BundleError` (`folder name must look like case-001`); an entry with a valid name that is not `case-NNN` is still a `BundleError`.
+  - **`signer.key_path` resolves the whole target, checks the shape of the key folder before it makes any folder and the key file by `pathguard.inside` right after; its messages hold no path.** `signer.load_private_key` and `load_public_key` raise `PathRefused` (a `ConfigError`) for a missing file or a folder (on origin/main: `FileNotFoundError` and `IsADirectoryError`, both holding the path).
+  - **The failure audit code of `score` when the report name is a folder is `PathRefused`** (on origin/main it was `IsADirectoryError`).
+  - **`LocalDirSink` refuses an event id that is not one plain file name, such as one with `:` or `?`.** On origin/main such an id was written to a file of that name. `write_intent` now raises `AuditUnavailable` without the id in the text. `write_outcome` drops the event only for such an id: the audit record of that outcome is NOT written, and the only trace is the `refused_events` counter and one warning without the id. A refusal for any other reason (for example the objects folder has become a file) is not dropped: the record takes the same outbox path as any other disk error. A corrupt outbox file whose `corrupt` folder is a link stays where it is for a person, and a link inside the outbox is skipped, so reconcile never moves or removes the file it points to.
+  - **When `replay` stops after the output folder exists, it tries to write `FAILED.txt`, also into an empty folder.** This covers an error, a refusal and Ctrl-C. The marker is written only if the output folder is still a folder; the folder is checked again with `pathguard.operator_dir` and the fixed file name is written without a second strict join. If that write fails, the original error is still the one raised, with a note (`FAILED.txt was not written (<error name>)`). Ctrl-C and an out-of-memory error are not swallowed: they propagate, with a note that names the first error.
+  - **`hashing.hash_files`, `FakeLLM`, `guard.judge.qualify` and the `mounted_file` secrets folder now go through `pathguard`.** `FakeLLM` also checks the recording file name, not only its folder.
+  - **One last check right before each read.** The answers file, the replay digest file, `hash_files`, the `mounted_file` secrets reader, the signer key files, the judge golden file and the Vault sign-in token file all use one pattern, `pathguard.join` called as `pathguard.join(<folder>, <name>, ..., must_exist=True, kind="file")` (the file must exist and be a file; one guard per read, none doubled), in the 14 lines before the read (the refusal handling sits between), and only the returned path is read. Two differences, stated so the entry does not claim more: `hash_files` and the secrets reader first fold the name with `config.confined` (so `a/../b` still works) and then give each name that is left to `pathguard.join`; the Vault sign-in token file uses `pathguard.join(<folder>, <name>, what=...)` without `must_exist` and `kind`, so that a missing token file keeps its old message `(FileNotFoundError)` and the read itself finds a folder or a missing file. A folder typed on the command line keeps `operator_dir` (nothing is read from it by name).
+  - **Errors in the cause-list, bundle and hashing checks are raised without a chained earlier error**, so a traceback does not print the earlier error text.
+  - **Suppression comments.** The Sonar exemption comments in `k1b/score.py` and `cli.py` are gone, and a test fails if one appears in `src` or `tests`, in any letter case. The files this change created or rewrote in full hold no suppression of any kind (a test lists them). The other files it edited (`config.py`, `cli.py`, `audit/local_dir.py`, `audit/signer.py`, `actions/secrets_vault.py`, `registry.py`, `tools.py`) keep the suppressions they had; a test holds each to the count it had on origin/main, so none can be added.
+  - **What this does not claim.** It does not claim that any SonarCloud finding is cleared: the code scanner may still report a folder typed on the command line, because nothing can "contain" a path the operator chose; the owner decides in SonarQube. The checks assume hostile text, not a second local process that changes the folder while a command runs. Not covered: Windows paths longer than 260 characters, hard links, mount points, Windows junctions and `\\?\` paths (the name rules run on every system; the junction behaviour could not be run on the Linux machine used to build this). The demo repositories are not changed by this entry and still hold unreviewed path sites.
 - **Breaking: the first stage is now called `demo`; `environment: dev` is refused.** The stages are exactly `demo`, `pilot` and `prod`. A config that says `environment: dev` stops at start with "environment: dev was renamed to demo; write environment: demo (allowed values: demo, pilot, prod)" (shown beside any other problem found in the same file, and also for `Dev`, `DEV` and spellings with spaces, which are refused all the same); any other value (`Demo`, `staging`, `uat`, an empty value) is refused too, and values are never tidied or guessed. To upgrade: (1) change `environment:` in your config to `demo`; (2) if your code imports `backends.table.DEV_ONLY`, import `DEMO_ONLY` instead (same six kind pairs, no old name kept); (3) a project label such as `uat` is not a stage: map it to one stage in your own config. Also: the pilot and prod checks (`deferred.py`, `registry.py`, `app.py`) now ask one function, `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_live(environment)`, which treats anything that is not exactly `demo` as live; the prod-only rules (no local key file, a retention-agreed anchor) ask `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_prod(environment)`, which is true for `prod` and for any value that is not a stage. `STAGES`, `is_live` and `is_prod` live in the new leaf module `hadavin_sdk/stages.py`, not in `config.py`; the list `deferred.LIVE` is removed. The redactor no longer sweeps the words `demo` and `pilot` as a name (it already skipped `prod`), but a secret that reads `demo` is still swept. No stage is stored today; the first change that stores one must map an old `dev` value to `demo` on read, for display only and never for a guard decision, and add a test through that real read site.
 - **Client-profile check: two new refusals.**
   - **Test objects are refused in pilot and prod.** A pilot or prod `app.build` call that passes `secrets=`, `channel=`, `detector=`, `llm=` or `clock=` (objects that stand in for a configured part and are meant for tests) now stops with a `ConfigError` before anything is built, unless the caller also passes `allow_test_doubles=True`.
